# Phase 2 — Content Layer: Reading Markdown Files

## 🎯 What You Will Accomplish

Write the utility functions that read `.md` files from disk, parse their frontmatter, and return **typed data** your components can use safely. Create two real sample posts so you have actual content to work with.

---

## 💡 Core Concepts

### Node.js `fs` in a Server Component
Because Next.js runs server components on Node.js, you can use the built-in `fs` module to read files directly from disk. This code:
- **Never runs in the browser** — only on the server at request time (or build time)
- **Has no performance cost** on the client — zero JavaScript is shipped for file reads
- **Replaces a database** for static content that changes infrequently

### `process.cwd()`
In Node.js, `process.cwd()` returns the **current working directory** — always the project root when running `next dev` or `next build`. Use `path.join(process.cwd(), 'content', 'posts')` to get an absolute path that works on any machine.

### Static Generation vs SSR
Next.js can render pages in two modes:

| Mode | When | Use case |
|---|---|---|
| **Static (SSG)** | At build time | Blog posts — content doesn't change often |
| **Dynamic (SSR)** | At request time | Dashboards, user-specific pages |

For a blog, `generateStaticParams` + file reads = blazing-fast pre-rendered HTML.

### TypeScript interfaces
Defining a `Post` interface means TypeScript will catch every typo in frontmatter access (`post.titel` → ❌ error) before it becomes a runtime bug.

---

## 🛠 Step-by-Step Instructions

### Step 1 · Define the Post interface

Create `lib/posts.ts` with the type definitions first:

```typescript
import fs from 'fs';
import path from 'path';
import matter from 'gray-matter';

// Absolute path to the content directory
const POSTS_DIR = path.join(process.cwd(), 'content', 'posts');

// Metadata only — used for the post list
export interface PostMeta {
  slug: string;
  title: string;
  date: string;       // ISO string: "2024-03-15"
  tags: string[];
  excerpt: string;
}

// Full post — metadata + Markdown body
export interface Post extends PostMeta {
  content: string;
}
```

### Step 2 · Write `getAllPosts()`

Add this function to `lib/posts.ts`:

```typescript
/**
 * Reads every .md file in content/posts/,
 * parses frontmatter, and returns metadata sorted newest-first.
 */
export function getAllPosts(): PostMeta[] {
  // 1. List all files in the directory
  const fileNames = fs.readdirSync(POSTS_DIR);

  const posts = fileNames
    .filter((name) => name.endsWith('.md'))   // only .md files
    .map((fileName) => {
      const slug = fileName.replace(/\.md$/, '');
      const rawFile = fs.readFileSync(
        path.join(POSTS_DIR, fileName),
        'utf8'
      );

      // gray-matter splits frontmatter (data) from body (content)
      const { data } = matter(rawFile);

      return {
        slug,
        title: data.title as string,
        date: data.date as string,
        tags: (data.tags as string[]) ?? [],
        excerpt: data.excerpt as string,
      } satisfies PostMeta;
    });

  // Sort by date — newest post first
  return posts.sort((a, b) => (a.date < b.date ? 1 : -1));
}
```

### Step 3 · Write `getPostBySlug()`

```typescript
/**
 * Reads a single .md file by slug and returns
 * the full post (metadata + Markdown content).
 */
export function getPostBySlug(slug: string): Post {
  const filePath = path.join(POSTS_DIR, `${slug}.md`);
  const rawFile = fs.readFileSync(filePath, 'utf8');
  const { data, content } = matter(rawFile);

  return {
    slug,
    title: data.title as string,
    date: data.date as string,
    tags: (data.tags as string[]) ?? [],
    excerpt: data.excerpt as string,
    content,   // raw Markdown — will be rendered in Phase 4
  };
}
```

### Step 4 · Create two sample posts

Create `content/posts/hello-world.md`:

```markdown
---
title: "Hello World"
date: "2024-03-15"
tags: ["meta", "blogging"]
excerpt: "Welcome to my new developer blog — why I'm starting to write."
---

# Hello World

After years of learning from other developers' blog posts, I decided to start my own.

This blog will cover **JavaScript**, **TypeScript**, **system design**, and anything else I find interesting.
```

Create `content/posts/understanding-closures.md`:

```markdown
---
title: "Understanding Closures in JavaScript"
date: "2024-03-22"
tags: ["javascript", "fundamentals"]
excerpt: "Closures are one of JavaScript's most powerful — and most misunderstood — features."
---

# Understanding Closures

A **closure** is a function that remembers variables from its outer scope,
even after the outer function has returned.

```javascript
function createCounter() {
  let count = 0;         // this variable is "closed over"
  return function () {
    return ++count;
  };
}

const counter = createCounter();
console.log(counter()); // 1
console.log(counter()); // 2
```

Each call to `counter()` increments the same `count` variable.
```

---

## ✅ Phase Checkpoint

- [ ] `lib/posts.ts` exports both `getAllPosts()` and `getPostBySlug()`
- [ ] Both `.md` files exist in `content/posts/` with correct frontmatter fields
- [ ] `getAllPosts()` returns 2 items sorted newest-first (March 22 before March 15)
- [ ] Each item has the fields: `slug`, `title`, `date`, `tags`, `excerpt`
- [ ] `getPostBySlug('hello-world')` returns the full Markdown content string

---

> **🧠 Key takeaway:** `gray-matter` gives you a clean split between structure (frontmatter metadata) and prose (Markdown content). TypeScript interfaces ensure you never access a field that doesn't exist.

# Phase 3 — Blog Layout & Routing

## 🎯 What You Will Accomplish

Build the homepage (post list) and the individual post page using Next.js App Router. Wire up static generation so every post becomes a pre-rendered HTML page at build time. Style the navigation and layout with Tailwind CSS.

---

## 💡 Core Concepts

### Server Components vs Client Components

| | Server Component | Client Component |
|---|---|---|
| **Default?** | ✅ Yes | ❌ No (needs `'use client'`) |
| **Can fetch/read files** | ✅ Yes | ❌ No |
| **Can use useState / hooks** | ❌ No | ✅ Yes |
| **JS sent to browser** | ❌ None | ✅ Yes |

**Rule of thumb:** only add `'use client'` when you need interactivity (click handlers, state, effects).

### `generateStaticParams`
This exported function tells Next.js which dynamic slugs to pre-render at **build time**.

```typescript
export async function generateStaticParams() {
  return getAllPosts().map((post) => ({ slug: post.slug }));
}
// Result: Next.js generates /blog/hello-world and /blog/understanding-closures
// as static HTML files — zero server load at runtime
```

### Layouts
`app/layout.tsx` wraps **every page** in the application. It's the right place for:
- The `<html>` and `<body>` tags
- Global navigation
- Google Fonts
- Meta tags

---

## 🛠 Step-by-Step Instructions

### Step 1 · Update the root layout

Replace `app/layout.tsx` with:

```tsx
import type { Metadata } from 'next';
import { Inter, Playfair_Display } from 'next/font/google';
import './globals.css';

const inter = Inter({
  subsets: ['latin'],
  variable: '--font-inter',
});

const playfair = Playfair_Display({
  subsets: ['latin'],
  variable: '--font-playfair',
});

export const metadata: Metadata = {
  title: { default: 'My Blog', template: '%s | My Blog' },
  description: 'A developer blog built with Next.js',
};

export default function RootLayout({
  children,
}: {
  children: React.ReactNode;
}) {
  return (
    <html lang="en" className={`${inter.variable} ${playfair.variable}`}>
      <body className="bg-stone-950 text-stone-100 font-sans antialiased">
        <header className="sticky top-0 z-50 border-b border-stone-800 bg-stone-950/80 backdrop-blur">
          <nav className="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 h-14 flex items-center justify-between">
            <a href="/" className="font-serif text-lg font-semibold tracking-tight">
              my blog
            </a>
            <span className="text-sm text-stone-500">writing on code & craft</span>
          </nav>
        </header>
        <main className="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-12">
          {children}
        </main>
      </body>
    </html>
  );
}
```

### Step 2 · Build the post list homepage

Replace `app/page.tsx`:

```tsx
import Link from 'next/link';
import { getAllPosts } from '@/lib/posts';

export default function HomePage() {
  const posts = getAllPosts();

  return (
    <div className="space-y-10">
      <header>
        <h1 className="font-serif text-4xl font-bold tracking-tight">
          Writing
        </h1>
        <p className="mt-2 text-stone-400">
          Notes on software, systems, and craft.
        </p>
      </header>

      <ul className="divide-y divide-stone-800">
        {posts.map((post) => (
          <li key={post.slug} className="py-6">
            <Link href={`/blog/${post.slug}`} className="group block">
              <div className="flex items-center gap-3 mb-1">
                <time className="text-sm text-stone-500">{post.date}</time>
                <div className="flex gap-1.5">
                  {post.tags.map((tag) => (
                    <span
                      key={tag}
                      className="text-xs px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-stone-800 text-stone-500"
                    >
                      {tag}
                    </span>
                  ))}
                </div>
              </div>
              <h2 className="font-serif text-xl font-semibold group-hover:text-amber-400 transition-colors">
                {post.title}
              </h2>
              <p className="mt-1 text-stone-400 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                {post.excerpt}
              </p>
            </Link>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </div>
  );
}
```

### Step 3 · Create the dynamic post page

Create `app/blog/[slug]/page.tsx`:

```tsx
import { getAllPosts, getPostBySlug } from '@/lib/posts';
import type { Metadata } from 'next';
import { notFound } from 'next/navigation';

interface PageProps {
  params: Promise<{ slug: string }>;
}

// Tell Next.js which slugs to statically generate
export async function generateStaticParams() {
  return getAllPosts().map((post) => ({ slug: post.slug }));
}

// Generate per-post SEO metadata
export async function generateMetadata({ params }: PageProps): Promise<Metadata> {
  const { slug } = await params;
  const post = getPostBySlug(slug);
  return { title: post.title, description: post.excerpt };
}

export default async function PostPage({ params }: PageProps) {
  const { slug } = await params;

  let post;
  try {
    post = getPostBySlug(slug);
  } catch {
    notFound(); // shows the 404 page
  }

  return (
    <article>
      <header className="mb-10">
        <time className="text-sm text-stone-500">{post!.date}</time>
        <h1 className="mt-2 font-serif text-4xl font-bold tracking-tight leading-tight">
          {post!.title}
        </h1>
        <div className="mt-3 flex gap-2">
          {post!.tags.map((tag) => (
            <span
              key={tag}
              className="text-xs px-2 py-0.5 rounded-full bg-stone-800 text-stone-400"
            >
              {tag}
            </span>
          ))}
        </div>
      </header>

      {/* Phase 4 will replace this with the Prose component */}
      <pre className="text-stone-300 whitespace-pre-wrap text-sm leading-relaxed">
        {post!.content}
      </pre>
    </article>
  );
}
```

---

## ✅ Phase Checkpoint

- [ ] The homepage at `/` renders a list of both posts
- [ ] Each post title links to `/blog/[slug]`
- [ ] Visiting `/blog/hello-world` shows the post with its title and tags
- [ ] Visiting `/blog/does-not-exist` returns a 404 page
- [ ] The navigation header is sticky and shows on all pages

---

> **🧠 Key takeaway:** `generateStaticParams` is what turns a dynamic route into a set of static HTML files. The blog becomes a CDN-hosted static site that loads instantly, without touching a server on each request.

# Phase 4 — Rendering Markdown & Syntax Highlighting

## 🎯 What You Will Accomplish

Replace the raw `<pre>` block with a beautiful, styled Markdown renderer. Handle headings, lists, blockquotes, tables, inline code, and fenced code blocks with full syntax highlighting. Build a reusable `Prose` component.

---

## 💡 Core Concepts

### The Remark → Rehype Pipeline

`react-markdown` processes Markdown through a two-stage plugin pipeline:

```
Markdown string
    ↓  remark (Markdown plugins)
Markdown AST
    ↓  remark-to-rehype
HTML AST
    ↓  rehype (HTML plugins)
HTML AST with classes
    ↓  react-markdown renderer
React elements
```

- **remark-gfm** — adds GitHub Flavored Markdown (tables, task lists, strikethrough, autolinks)
- **rehype-highlight** — walks code blocks in the HTML AST and adds `hljs` CSS classes for syntax colours

### Custom Components
`react-markdown` lets you replace any HTML element with your own React component:

```tsx
<ReactMarkdown
  components={{
    // All external links open in a new tab
    a: ({ href, children }) => (
      <a href={href} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
        {children}
      </a>
    ),
  }}
/>
```

---

## 🛠 Step-by-Step Instructions

### Step 1 · Import highlight.js theme

In `app/globals.css`, add at the top:

```css
@import 'highlight.js/styles/github-dark.css';
```

This loads the dark syntax theme for all code blocks.

### Step 2 · Create the Prose component

Create `components/Prose.tsx`:

```tsx
'use client';

import ReactMarkdown from 'react-markdown';
import remarkGfm from 'remark-gfm';
import rehypeHighlight from 'rehype-highlight';
import type { Components } from 'react-markdown';

// Override specific HTML elements
const mdComponents: Components = {
  // Open external links in a new tab
  a({ href, children, ...props }) {
    const isExternal = href?.startsWith('http');
    return (
      <a
        href={href}
        target={isExternal ? '_blank' : undefined}
        rel={isExternal ? 'noopener noreferrer' : undefined}
        {...props}
      >
        {children}
      </a>
    );
  },

  // Add a subtle top border to h2 headings
  h2({ children, ...props }) {
    return (
      <h2
        className="border-t border-stone-800 pt-8 mt-10"
        {...props}
      >
        {children}
      </h2>
    );
  },
};

export function Prose({ content }: { content: string }) {
  return (
    <div className="prose prose-invert prose-stone prose-headings:font-serif max-w-none">
      <ReactMarkdown
        remarkPlugins={[remarkGfm]}
        rehypePlugins={[rehypeHighlight]}
        components={mdComponents}
      >
        {content}
      </ReactMarkdown>
    </div>
  );
}
```

### Step 3 · Install Tailwind Typography

```bash
npm install @tailwindcss/typography
```

Add the plugin to `tailwind.config.ts`:

```typescript
import type { Config } from 'tailwindcss';

const config: Config = {
  content: [
    './app/**/*.{ts,tsx}',
    './components/**/*.{ts,tsx}',
  ],
  plugins: [require('@tailwindcss/typography')],
};

export default config;
```

### Step 4 · Wire Prose into the post page

Update `app/blog/[slug]/page.tsx` — replace the `<pre>` block:

```tsx
import { Prose } from '@/components/Prose';

// Replace the <pre> tag with:
<Prose content={post!.content} />
```

### Step 5 · Verify the output

Restart the dev server and visit `/blog/understanding-closures`. You should see:

- ✅ `# Understanding Closures` renders as a large serif heading
- ✅ `**bold text**` renders as bold
- ✅ The code block renders with syntax colours (keywords amber, strings green, comments grey)
- ✅ Tables (if any) render as proper HTML tables
- ✅ Links in the post open in a new tab

---

## ✅ Phase Checkpoint

- [ ] Both posts render full Markdown (headings, bold, code) — not raw text
- [ ] Code blocks have syntax highlighting with a dark theme
- [ ] The page looks good on mobile (max-width prose is readable)
- [ ] External links open in a new tab (`target="_blank"`)
- [ ] GFM tables render as proper `<table>` elements

---

> **🧠 Key takeaway:** The remark/rehype plugin pipeline is how the entire open-source Markdown ecosystem works — from GitHub to MDX to Docusaurus. Understanding it lets you extend any Markdown-based tool.

# Phase 2 — Database Schema & Prisma ORM

## 🎯 What You Will Accomplish

Connect a PostgreSQL database to your Express API using Prisma ORM. Define a schema with Users and Tasks, run migrations, seed initial data, and implement the full Task repository layer with type-safe CRUD operations.

---

## 💡 Core Concepts

### What is Prisma?

Prisma is an ORM (Object-Relational Mapper) that gives you:
1. **A schema file** (`schema.prisma`) — your database structure in code
2. **Migrations** — version-controlled SQL changes applied to the DB
3. **A generated TypeScript client** — type-safe database access (`prisma.user.findMany()`)

No raw SQL. No type gymnastics. Your schema IS your types.

### Schema Design

Our schema will have two tables:

```
User
  id        UUID (PK)
  email     unique
  password  hashed
  createdAt

Task
  id        UUID (PK)
  title
  completed Boolean
  userId    FK → User
  createdAt
  updatedAt
```

This is a one-to-many relationship: one User has many Tasks.

### Prisma Singleton

In development, Next.js/Node hot-reloading creates a new `PrismaClient` instance on every file save — exhausting the database connection pool. The singleton pattern prevents this:

```typescript
// Always use the same instance
const globalForPrisma = global as typeof global & { prisma?: PrismaClient };
export const prisma = globalForPrisma.prisma ?? new PrismaClient();
if (process.env.NODE_ENV !== 'production') globalForPrisma.prisma = prisma;
```

---

## 🛠 Step-by-Step Instructions

### Step 1 · Install Prisma

```bash
npm install prisma @prisma/client
npx prisma init
```

This creates:
- `prisma/schema.prisma` — the schema file
- Updates `.env` with a `DATABASE_URL` placeholder

### Step 2 · Write the schema

Replace `prisma/schema.prisma`:

```prisma
generator client {
  provider = "prisma-client-js"
}

datasource db {
  provider = "postgresql"
  url      = env("DATABASE_URL")
}

model User {
  id        String   @id @default(uuid())
  email     String   @unique
  password  String   // stores bcrypt hash, never plaintext
  tasks     Task[]   // relation: one user → many tasks
  createdAt DateTime @default(now())
}

model Task {
  id        String   @id @default(uuid())
  title     String
  completed Boolean  @default(false)
  user      User     @relation(fields: [userId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  userId    String
  createdAt DateTime @default(now())
  updatedAt DateTime @updatedAt
}
```

### Step 3 · Run the migration

```bash
npx prisma migrate dev --name init
```

This:
1. Generates a SQL migration file in `prisma/migrations/`
2. Applies it to the database
3. Regenerates the Prisma client

> **If you don't have PostgreSQL locally**, use [Supabase](https://supabase.com) free tier or [Neon](https://neon.tech) — copy the `DATABASE_URL` they give you into `.env`.

### Step 4 · Create the Prisma singleton

Create `src/config/prisma.ts`:

```typescript
import { PrismaClient } from '@prisma/client';

const globalForPrisma = global as typeof global & {
  prisma?: PrismaClient;
};

export const prisma =
  globalForPrisma.prisma ??
  new PrismaClient({
    log: process.env.NODE_ENV === 'development'
      ? ['query', 'error']
      : ['error'],
  });

if (process.env.NODE_ENV !== 'production') {
  globalForPrisma.prisma = prisma;
}
```

### Step 5 · Implement the Task repository

Create `src/repositories/task.repository.ts`:

```typescript
import { prisma } from '../config/prisma';

export const taskRepository = {
  // Find all tasks for a specific user
  async findAllByUser(userId: string) {
    return prisma.task.findMany({
      where: { userId },
      orderBy: { createdAt: 'desc' },
    });
  },

  // Find a single task (owned by this user)
  async findOne(id: string, userId: string) {
    return prisma.task.findFirst({
      where: { id, userId },  // scoped to the user — prevents data leaks
    });
  },

  // Create a new task
  async create(userId: string, title: string) {
    return prisma.task.create({
      data: { title, userId },
    });
  },

  // Update title or completion status
  async update(
    id: string,
    userId: string,
    data: { title?: string; completed?: boolean }
  ) {
    return prisma.task.update({
      where: { id },
      data,
    });
  },

  // Delete a task (with ownership check)
  async delete(id: string, userId: string) {
    return prisma.task.deleteMany({
      where: { id, userId },  // deleteMany for where clause with multiple conditions
    });
  },
};
```

### Step 6 · Seed test data

Create `prisma/seed.ts`:

```typescript
import { PrismaClient } from '@prisma/client';
import bcrypt from 'bcryptjs';

const prisma = new PrismaClient();

async function main() {
  const hashed = await bcrypt.hash('password123', 12);

  const user = await prisma.user.upsert({
    where: { email: 'test@example.com' },
    update: {},
    create: {
      email: 'test@example.com',
      password: hashed,
      tasks: {
        create: [
          { title: 'Set up the project' },
          { title: 'Implement authentication' },
          { title: 'Write the task endpoints' },
        ],
      },
    },
    include: { tasks: true },
  });

  console.log('Seeded user:', user.email, 'with', user.tasks.length, 'tasks');
}

main()
  .catch(console.error)
  .finally(() => prisma.$disconnect());
```

Add to `package.json`:

```json
"prisma": {
  "seed": "ts-node prisma/seed.ts"
}
```

Run it:

```bash
npm install bcryptjs @types/bcryptjs
npx prisma db seed
```

---

## ✅ Phase Checkpoint

- [ ] `npx prisma migrate dev` runs without errors
- [ ] `npx prisma studio` shows the `User` and `Task` tables (open at localhost:5555)
- [ ] The seed script creates 1 user and 3 tasks
- [ ] `taskRepository.findAllByUser(userId)` returns tasks scoped to that user
- [ ] Prisma's generated types appear in your editor's autocomplete

---

> **🧠 Key takeaway:** Prisma's type safety goes both ways — the schema defines the database shape, and the generated client ensures TypeScript knows every field name, type, and relationship. A typo in a query is a compile error, not a runtime crash.

# Phase 3 — Authentication with JWT

## 🎯 What You Will Accomplish

Build a complete authentication system: user registration, login, and a JWT middleware that protects routes. Passwords are hashed with bcrypt. Tokens are signed with HS256 and validated on every protected request.

---

## 💡 Core Concepts

### JWT (JSON Web Token) Flow

```
1. User sends email + password
        ↓
2. Server verifies password against bcrypt hash
        ↓
3. Server signs a JWT: { userId, email } + secret
        ↓
4. Client stores the JWT (localStorage or httpOnly cookie)
        ↓
5. Client sends JWT in every request: Authorization: Bearer <token>
        ↓
6. Middleware verifies signature → extracts userId → attaches to req.user
        ↓
7. Controller knows who made the request without touching the database
```

### Why bcrypt?

Plain SHA-256 is **not** safe for passwords:
- It's fast — attackers can try billions of hashes per second
- bcrypt is deliberately slow (the "work factor" is configurable)
- It adds a random salt, so identical passwords produce different hashes

```typescript
const hash = await bcrypt.hash(password, 12); // 12 = work factor
const isValid = await bcrypt.compare(plaintext, hash);
```

### Augmenting Express's `Request` type

To add `req.user` to every request without TypeScript errors:

```typescript
// src/types/express.d.ts
declare namespace Express {
  interface Request {
    user?: { id: string; email: string };
  }
}
```

---

## 🛠 Step-by-Step Instructions

### Step 1 · Install dependencies

```bash
npm install jsonwebtoken bcryptjs
npm install -D @types/jsonwebtoken @types/bcryptjs
```

### Step 2 · Augment the Request type

Create `src/types/express.d.ts`:

```typescript
declare global {
  namespace Express {
    interface Request {
      user?: {
        id: string;
        email: string;
      };
    }
  }
}

export {};
```

### Step 3 · Create the auth service

Create `src/services/auth.service.ts`:

```typescript
import bcrypt from 'bcryptjs';
import jwt from 'jsonwebtoken';
import { prisma } from '../config/prisma';
import { env } from '../config/env';

export const authService = {
  async register(email: string, password: string) {
    // 1. Check for existing account
    const existing = await prisma.user.findUnique({ where: { email } });
    if (existing) {
      throw new Error('EMAIL_TAKEN');
    }

    // 2. Hash the password — never store plaintext
    const hashed = await bcrypt.hash(password, 12);

    // 3. Create the user
    const user = await prisma.user.create({
      data: { email, password: hashed },
    });

    // 4. Return a signed token
    return signToken(user.id, user.email);
  },

  async login(email: string, password: string) {
    // 1. Find the user
    const user = await prisma.user.findUnique({ where: { email } });
    if (!user) {
      throw new Error('INVALID_CREDENTIALS');
    }

    // 2. Verify the password
    const valid = await bcrypt.compare(password, user.password);
    if (!valid) {
      // Return same error — don't reveal whether email exists
      throw new Error('INVALID_CREDENTIALS');
    }

    // 3. Return a signed token
    return signToken(user.id, user.email);
  },
};

function signToken(userId: string, email: string): string {
  return jwt.sign(
    { userId, email },
    env.JWT_SECRET,
    { expiresIn: '7d' }
  );
}
```

### Step 4 · Build the JWT middleware

Create `src/middleware/authenticate.ts`:

```typescript
import { Request, Response, NextFunction } from 'express';
import jwt from 'jsonwebtoken';
import { env } from '../config/env';

interface JwtPayload {
  userId: string;
  email: string;
}

export function authenticate(
  req: Request,
  res: Response,
  next: NextFunction
) {
  // Extract token from Authorization header
  const authHeader = req.headers.authorization;
  const token = authHeader?.startsWith('Bearer ')
    ? authHeader.slice(7)
    : null;

  if (!token) {
    res.status(401).json({ error: 'Authentication required' });
    return;
  }

  try {
    const payload = jwt.verify(token, env.JWT_SECRET) as JwtPayload;
    req.user = { id: payload.userId, email: payload.email };
    next();
  } catch {
    res.status(401).json({ error: 'Invalid or expired token' });
  }
}
```

